    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Captured as a boy in the Ming conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and castrated, Ma He rose in the household of Zhu Di and helped him seize the throne as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> in 1402.</li><li>As admiral he led seven voyages from 1405 to 1433 that reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast, mixing trade and gifts with displays and use of military force.</li><li>The voyages ended under later emperors and officials played them down for centuries, until Liang Qichao's biography of 1904 made Zheng He widely known.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A giraffe Zheng He brought back from Malindi was taken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to be a qilin, a sign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> upon the emperor's rule.</p></aside>
    </section>

